.TH LIBVIRT-BACKUP 1 "@DATE_MAN@" "libvirt-backup @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
libvirt-backup \- back up and restore the configuration of a libvirt/QEMU host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B libvirt-backup
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Backs up the configuration of a virtualization host (libvirt definitions, networks, pools, UEFI NVRAM and TPM
state, AppArmor profiles, systemd units, VFIO/GRUB/initramfs settings, local scripts) into
.B libvirt-config-*.tar.gz
archives, and restores it with the original owners, modes and ACLs.
The tool itself is part of every archive, so a bare machine can be restored from a single archive.
.PP
Commands that need root re-run themselves through
.BR sudo (8)
when started from a terminal. The graphical interface,
.BR libvirt-backup-gui ,
uses
.BR pkexec (1).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B backup \fR[\fB\-\-if\-changed\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-full\fR]
Create an archive now. The archive holds the libvirt configuration, hooks and local scripts, not disks.
By default (\fBlean_backup\fR) compiled executables and files over 1 MiB in /usr/local/sbin and /usr/local/bin,
and any file over 16 MiB elsewhere, are left out; the skipped files are listed.
\fB\-\-full\fR archives everything for this run; \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR shows what would be archived
(per group: files, size, skipped) and writes nothing. The duration of each phase is printed.
In a terminal a progress gauge (\fB[\(**\(**\(**\(**----] 42 %\fR) shows how far the backup is; with \fB\-\-json\fR the same
progress is sent as \fBprogress\fR events (a \fBfraction\fR between 0 and 1).
.TP
.B list
List the archives of the backup folder.
.TP
.B info \fIARCHIVE\fR
Show an archive's content by group and its embedded README.
.TP
.B verify \fR[\fIARCHIVE\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR]
Check integrity (full gzip CRC) and format.
.TP
.B delete \fIARCHIVE\fR... [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Permanently delete archives of the backup folder (numbers from
.BR list ,
names or \fBlatest\fR). No other file can be deleted. Interactive confirmation word: DELETE
(SUPPRIMER, L\(:OSCHEN or ELIMINAR in French, German or Spanish; SUPPRIMER is accepted in every language);
without a terminal \fB\-\-yes\fR is required. The \fIlibvirt-config-latest.tar.gz\fR link follows the newest
remaining archive, or is removed when none is left. Refused while a backup or restore is running.
.TP
.B restore \fR[\fIARCHIVE\fR|\fB\-\-latest\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIG,...\fR] [\fB\-\-skip\fR \fIG,...\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Restore (merge, nothing is deleted) after creating a safety archive.
Interactive confirmation word: RESTORE (RESTAURER, WIEDERHERSTELLEN or RESTAURAR in French, German or Spanish;
RESTAURER is accepted in every language).
.I ARCHIVE
is a number from
.BR list ,
a file name or any path (USB drive, copy...).
.TP
.B install \fR[\fB\-\-user\fR \fIU\fR] [\fB\-\-retention\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-first\-backup\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Create the settings and the watcher service, ask where archives go, make a first backup.
.TP
.B uninstall \fR[\fB\-\-purge\fR]
Remove the watcher service; with \fB\-\-purge\fR also the settings. Archives are never removed.
.TP
.B service \fR{status,start,stop,restart,enable,disable}
Drive the watcher service (libvirt-config-backup-watch.service).
.TP
.B status
Installation, service and archives.
.TP
.B config \fR{show|set \fIKEY=VALUE\fR...}
Settings: backup_user, backup_dir, retention, safety_retention, debounce, lean_backup, language
(\fBsystem\fR, \fBen\fR, \fBfr\fR, \fBde\fR or \fBes\fR).
.TP
.B groups
List the restorable groups: libvirt, nvram, apparmor, systemd, scripts, boot, network, storage, tool.
.TP
.B gui
Open the GTK4 interface (package libvirt-backup-gui).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-backup\-dir " DIR"
Folder of the archives for this run only (persistent with
.B install
and
.BR "config set" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-language " LANGUAGE"
Language of the messages for this run: \fBsystem\fR, \fBen\fR, \fBfr\fR, \fBde\fR or \fBes\fR.
Without this option the language is taken from the \fBLIBVIRT_BACKUP_LANG\fR variable, then from the
\fBlanguage\fR setting, then from the system language (\fBLC_ALL\fR, \fBLC_MESSAGES\fR, \fBLANG\fR,
\fBLANGUAGE\fR); English is used when none of them is translated.
.TP
.B \-\-json
One JSON event per line on standard output: \fBstep\fR, \fBprogress\fR, \fBinfo\fR, \fBwarn\fR, \fBerror\fR,
\fBresult\fR, \fBbusy\fR and \fBend\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LIBVIRT_BACKUP_LANG
Language of the messages (same values as \fB\-\-language\fR), overridden by that option.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/libvirt-backup/config.json
Settings. An empty backup_dir means the "virtualisation" folder of the backup user.
.TP
.I ~/.config/libvirt-backup/gui.json
Preferences of the graphical interface for one user (language of the window).
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/libvirt-config-backup-watch.service
Watcher service, created by
.BR "libvirt-backup install" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, non-zero on error.
